    "https://conncoll.peopleadmin.com/postings/1652": {
      "title": "Director of Information Security",
      "description": "Connecticut College Portal | Director of Information Security Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help About Connecticut College Director of Information Security Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Position Details Position Information Position Title Director of Information Security Department Information Services - Office of VP -Group Pay Type Exempt Appointment Type (A) Continuing Full-Time Hours Per Week 37.5 Number of weeks 52 weeks per year other - # of weeks 52 Benefits Eligible Full Benefits Geographical National Qualifies for Relocation Reimbursement No Work Schedule Job Description General Scope of Duties The Director of Information Security provides strategic vision, leadership, and institutional oversight for the College’s information security program. Reporting directly to the Vice President for Information Services and Librarian of the College ( CIO ), this position is responsible for protecting the College’s digital assets, managing institutional risk, and ensuring regulatory compliance in an increasingly complex threat environment. This is an ideal role for a collaborative security leader who is ready to shape an enterprise-wide program, whether as a first director-level opportunity or as a seasoned practitioner drawn to the mission and complexity of a liberal arts college. This position is in-person with potential to work remotely up to two days; In-person collaboration will be essential for key events and meetings. The Director partners with the VP to translate technical risks for non-technical institutional leaders, fosters a culture of security awareness across the campus community and implements a multi-year security modernization roadmap derived from recent institutional assessments supporting our transition to cloud-first infrastructure and modern identity management. They should endeavor to build an information security program that is designed to protect and enable, not restrict, the open inquiry and academic freedom that define the Connecticut College experience. This is done as a key member of the L&IT leadership team, working in a highly collaborative peer network alongside the Deputy CIO , Assistant Director of Network and Systems Infrastructure, Director of Computer Support Services, and the L&IT Project Manager to integrate security across all campus technology operations. The Director supervises the Senior Systems and Information Security Administrator. About Library & Information Technology at Connecticut College Library & Information Technology (L&IT) at Connecticut College is where librarians, technologists, archivists, media specialists, and everything in between come together as one team. We manage the systems that run the College, the network that connects it, the library that anchors it, and the research support that makes academic excellence possible. Whether someone needs help finding a rare archival document, troubleshooting a classroom projector five minutes before class, or thinking through the future of AI on a liberal arts campus, that’s us. And we don’t work in a bubble. Our staff regularly connect with colleagues at peer institutions through consortia like the Boston Library Consortium, the Oberlin Group, CLAC , and NERCOMP , because we believe the best ideas rarely stay inside one campus. Why You’ll Love It Here (And Why We’ll Love You) Our philosophy is pretty simple: We hire naturally curious people, give them room to be brilliant, and then get out of their way. We treat belonging and inclusion as the foundation of our work, not a footnote. We value collaboration over control, and we believe that while the work is serious, we absolutely do not have to take ourselves too seriously. If you can work hard, lead with curiosity, tolerate the occasional Swedish-inspired coffee break (look up fika), and agree that laughter is a legitimate workplace requirement, you’ll fit right in. General Duties and Responsibilities Primary Duties and Responsibilities Strengthen and scale the College’s enterprise-wide information security program, translating findings from recent institutional assessments into a forward-looking strategic roadmap. Lead the ongoing development and maintenance of information security policies, standards, and procedures that reflect an evolving threat landscape Serve as a high-level advocate for information security across the institution. Serve as the institutional steward of the College’s ‘Protect the Herd’ cybersecurity awareness initiative, evolving it as a campus-wide cultural program that reflects the College’s values and community. Conduct ongoing risk assessments and vulnerability audits to identify and mitigate potential threats. Ensure the College remains compliant with relevant state and federal regulations and applicable data privacy requirements. Serve as the primary point of contact for external security audits, cyber insurance requirements, and regulatory inquiries. Chair the cross-functional Information Security Team, facilitating regular collaboration between networking, security, computer support services, and IT leadership to ensure a unified approach to institutional risk. Oversee the detection, investigation, and remediation of security incidents. Supervise the Senior Systems and Information Security Administrator, ensuring that technical security controls including firewalls, EDR , MDM , and access controls are effectively managed and aligned with strategic goals. Manage the information security budget and strategically leverage security vendors and managed service providers (such as external vSOC, Managed SIEM , or specialized tools) to extend the capacity of the campus security program. Secondary Duties and Responsibilities • Contribute to the development and ongoing refinement of institutional guidelines for the secure and responsible use of emerging technologies, including generative AI tools and agentic AI, in collaboration with the AI@Conn initiative; serve on the internal L&IT AI working group • Contribute to divisional budget planning and resource prioritization in alignment with the College’s budget process • Support the VP in developing and presenting security strategy and risk posture updates to the President’s Cabinet and Board of Trustees • Contribute to policy development related to data governance, information privacy, accessibility, and digital equity Tertiary Duties and Responsibilities • Engage in professional development by staying current with emerging security threats, technologies, and best practices within the higher education sector; participate in professional organizations including EDUCAUSE , REN - ISAC , CEN , NERCOMP , and relevant peer consortia • As appropriate, participate in the life of the College by attending campus events such as L&IT speakers and events, athletic games, gallery openings, arts performances, student exhibits, and other signature experiences • Other duties or responsibilities as may be assigned Education and Skills Bachelor’s degree in a related field, OR an equivalent combination of certifications and significant progressive IT/security leadership experience At least seven years of progressive experience in information technology and security, with demonstrated experience leading projects, initiatives, or teams. Demonstrated ability to lead through ambiguity, including navigating evolving threat landscapes, organizational change, and institutional decision-making processes that are still in formation Proven experience with risk management frameworks such as NIST and regulatory compliance within a complex organizational environment; higher education experience is a significant plus Exceptional communication skills with the ability to build relationships and influence stakeholders at all levels of the organization, including non-technical audiences Professional certifications such as CISSP , CISM , or CISA are desirable Demonstrated ability to lead and develop technical staff while maintaining a strategic, institutional perspective Demonstrated commitment to belonging and inclusion, with experience building and leading teams that reflect a broad range of backgrounds, perspectives, and professional experiences Familiarity with identity and access management, data loss prevention, endpoint security, and cloud security principles A service-oriented mindset with the ability to build trust and credibility across the campus community Intellectual curiosity and a genuine commitment to the collaborative, mission-driven environment of a liberal arts college Preferred Qualifications Physical Demands The physical demands and work environment described here are representative of those that must be met by an employee to successfully perform the essential functions of this position. Reasonable accommodations may be made to enable individuals with disabilities to perform the essential functions. • Ability to remain in a stationary position for extended periods while working at a computer or in meetings • Frequent movement within office and campus environments; ability to traverse campus and move between multiple floors and buildings • Ability to lift and carry up to 25 pounds occasionally • Repetitive motions including typing, using a mouse, and handling documents • Ability to communicate effectively in person, by phone, and electronically • Visual and auditory ability to complete job-related tasks Driving Required No Salary Range $105,000-$130,000 Note Connecticut College is committed to fair and competitive compensation. The final offer will reflect the candidate’s experience, education, and the institution’s pay structure to ensure internal equity. Applicant Credentialing Thorough applicant credentialing will be conducted on the selected candidate which will include criminal records check, DMV check and depending on the position psychological exam and pre-placement physical. Posting Detail Information Open Date 08/06/2026 Applications accepted through Open Until Filled Yes Documents needed to Apply Required Documents Resume Cover Letter Optional Documents Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). Are you eligible to work in the U.S.? Yes No Will you now or in the future require sponsorship for employment visa status (e.g., H-1B visa)? Yes No To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://conncoll.peopleadmin.com/postings/1748": {
      "title": "Part Time Evening Athletic Center Coordinator",
      "description": "Connecticut College Portal | Part Time Evening Athletic Center Coordinator Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help About Connecticut College Part Time Evening Athletic Center Coordinator Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Position Details Position Information Position Title Part Time Evening Athletic Center Coordinator Department Athletic Center -Group Pay Type Non Exempt Appointment Type (A) Continuing Part-Time Hours Per Week 5 Number of weeks Other (Benefits Ineligible) other - # of weeks 40 Benefits Eligible No Benefits Geographical Regional Qualifies for Relocation Reimbursement No Work Schedule Thursday 5:00-10:00; On Call opportunities weekdays and weekends Job Description General Scope of Duties We are seeking a dependable, personable individual for a part-time evening position overseeing operations at the Athletic Center, with a primary schedule of Thursdays from 5:00 p.m. – 10:00 p.m. with additional opportunities available on other evenings and weekends as an on call. This role coordinates the use of the multi-purpose courts in the Charles B. Luce Field House, provides oversight to Athletic Center monitors, manages facility access and scheduling, and ensures the facility is properly monitored and secured at closing. The ideal candidate has excellent communication and interpersonal skills, enjoys working with students and other authorized facility users, and is looking for a flexible part-time opportunity. General Duties and Responsibilities Assist and provide direction to student employees to ensure controlled access to the facility. Work with athletic center manager to communicate daily schedule to users. Work with operations director on daily student monitor schedule and attendance. Execute the daily schedule for the athletic center spaces by communication with each group. Ensure the safety and comfort of all affiliated users by walk-throughs and intermittent ID checks. Meet periodically with director of athletics and/or athletic center manager and/or operations director to discuss supervision strategies. Demonstrate ethical behavior in regards to work duties and in relationships with students and members of the campus community. Maintain a safe, clean environment in all facilities. Follow athletic center closing protocols and collaborate with Campus Safety to secure the facilities each night. Education and Skills Bachelor’s degree (preferred) with strong consideration given to those who have a degree in a related field. 3+ years of demonstrated experience with similar responsibly a plus. Current certification in CPR and First Aide, will train. Ability to react calmly and effectively in an emergency situation Ability to interact well with a variety of people from all aspects of the College and community Preferred Qualifications Physical Demands Walkup flight of stairs; bending, squatting Driving Required No Salary Range $18.58 Note Connecticut College is committed to fair and competitive compensation. The final offer will reflect the candidate’s experience, education, and the institution’s pay structure to ensure internal equity. Applicant Credentialing Thorough applicant credentialing will be conducted on the selected candidate which will include criminal records check, DMV check and depending on the position psychological exam and pre-placement physical. Posting Detail Information Open Date 09/12/2023 Applications accepted through Open Until Filled Yes Documents needed to Apply Required Documents Resume Cover Letter Optional Documents Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). Are you eligible to work in the U.S.? Yes No Do you have a valid drivers license? Yes No To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://conncoll.peopleadmin.com/postings/1760": {
      "title": "Groundskeeper",
      "description": "Connecticut College Portal | Groundskeeper Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help About Connecticut College Groundskeeper Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Position Details Position Information Position Title Groundskeeper Department Grounds Maintenance -Group Pay Type Non Exempt Appointment Type (A) Continuing Full-Time Hours Per Week 40 Number of weeks 52 weeks per year other - # of weeks 52 Benefits Eligible Full Benefits Geographical Regional Qualifies for Relocation Reimbursement No Work Schedule Job Description General Scope of Duties Under the direction of the Landscaping Services Director, perform highly skilled trades duties in a variety of landscape and hardscape work. Performs skilled maintenance and/or construction work requiring the application of trade skills, codes, and standard trade practices of College historical campus, waterfront, and athletic fields. Makes estimates of time, personnel, and material required on assigned tasks; keeps necessary records; performs duties related to the trade or trade areas as required. These positions are categorized as Emergency Support Services/Essential Staff. Must be willing and able to work during emergency closures/delayed openings due to inclement weather or for significant events that affect campus operations. Applicants must also be willing to respond to emergencies on overtime (nights, weekends or holidays). These positions also require participation in the Weekly On-Call Rotation Schedule; when assigned to the on-call rotation, calls must be responded to during the on-call assignment within 15 minutes. General Duties and Responsibilities Priority Duties and Responsibilities: Prepares, installs and maintains decorative landscaping features to include flowers, plants, shrubs, ornamental grasses, trees, waterscapes, lighting and decorative and synthetic or natural materials in formal gardens, plant containers, borders and island beds. Clears brush, debris and leaves from lawns and catch basins Hauls materials, fills, grades and slopes work sites; pulls weeds, mulches, repairs drip lines, waters and fertilizes. Installs and maintains turf by seeding or siding, repairing, mowing and fertilizing. Installs and maintains recreational sports fields. General Duties and Responsibilities: . Installs, maintains and repairs asphalt pavement and other materials on traveled surfaces. Operates and maintains various large and small power and construction equipment including bucket/boom truck, front loader, backhoes, bulldozers, forklifts, tractors, dump trucks of five (5) ton or greater capacity, plow and pickup trucks and trailers, street sweepers, leaf vacuums, wood chippers, mowers, leaf blowers, weed whackers and chainsaws. Maintains and repairs small gas engine power equipment and large equipment to include, but not limited to, oil and spark plug change, blade sharpening, tire change or repair, fluid change and battery replacement. Uses and maintains various hand tools; assists with the setup and dismantling of, but not limited to, plantings, fences, chairs, tents, bleachers and tables for special events; installs and maintains fences, bollards and signage campus wide. Performs pre-treatment and snow and ice removal. Assists arborists with tree pruning or felling operations. Maintains orderliness and efficiency of landscape work sheds and surrounding areas. Removes trash and changes bins daily. Ensure all work processes comply with OSHA requirements and regulations, college policies and procedures including tracking work orders and service orders assigned to the department. May direct student and/or temporary workers and/or contracted services as needed and assigned. May apply pesticides under direct supervision. May paint poles and other structures within the landscape. Performs related duties as required. Education and Skills 4-7 years of general experience in a particular trade area. A combination of education and experience that provides a demonstrated knowledge of various plants used in landscape features; knowledge of proper care of plants for sustainment, principles, methods, materials, tools, and equipment. Basic knowledge of turf grass management practices. Basic knowledge of pesticides and application methods and techniques. Interpersonal skills; oral and written communication skills. Ability to keep shop records, interpret and read blueprints. Ability to utilize computer software ( CMMS ). Must possess and maintain a valid driver’s license. Preferred Qualifications Physical Demands Driving Required Yes Salary Range 23.49 Note Connecticut College is committed to fair and competitive compensation. The final offer will reflect the candidate’s experience, education, and the institution’s pay structure to ensure internal equity. Applicant Credentialing Thorough applicant credentialing will be conducted on the selected candidate which will include criminal records check, DMV check and depending on the position psychological exam and pre-placement physical. Posting Detail Information Open Date 08/19/2026 Applications accepted through Open Until Filled Yes Documents needed to Apply Required Documents Resume Optional Documents Cover Letter Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). Are you eligible to work in the U.S.? Yes No Do you have a valid drivers license? Yes No Will you now or in the future require sponsorship for employment visa status (e.g., H-1B visa)? Yes No To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://conncoll.peopleadmin.com/postings/1773": {
      "title": "Assistant Director for Residential Life, South Campus",
      "description": "Connecticut College Portal | Assistant Director for Residential Life, South Campus Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help About Connecticut College Assistant Director for Residential Life, South Campus Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Position Details Position Information Position Title Assistant Director for Residential Life, South Campus Department Residential Life -Group Pay Type Exempt Appointment Type (A) Continuing Full-Time Hours Per Week 37.5 Number of weeks 52 weeks per year other - # of weeks 52 Benefits Eligible Full Benefits Geographical Regional Qualifies for Relocation Reimbursement No Work Schedule Job Description General Scope of Duties The Assistant Director for Campus Life, South Campus, plays a vital role in creating a welcoming, inclusive, and supportive residential experience for approximately 950 undergraduate students across more than 13 unique residential communities. Through the supervision and development of approximately 30 paraprofessional staff, this position fosters a strong sense of belonging, promotes student safety and well-being, and helps build vibrant communities where students can thrive both personally and academically. General Duties and Responsibilities Priority Duties and Responsibilities: 1. Oversee the daily supervision, development, training and support for a student staff (RAs. RC) team. 2. Identify and coordinate response for facilities related needs within residential communities. 3. Support and monitor the programming efforts for designated areas, to ensure campus engagement occurs, regularly. 4. Participate in the planning, development and facilitation of regular in-service, training and student staff development. 5. Support the daily functions and operations of Residential Life and Campus Life General Duties and Responsibilities: 1. Provide administrative oversight, leadership, and community development to the South Campus area. 2. Hire, train, supervise, develop and evaluate student staff. 3. Maintain frequent and regular contact with the student residence hall staff through individual meetings, area meetings, e-mail, and phone contact. 4. Develop healthy, engaged and inclusive communities by fostering a supportive environment focusing on student development. 5. Assist students in academic and social adjustment to Connecticut College; assist in developing individual responsibility, personal growth and social awareness. 6. Work collaboratively in the development of residential learning communities. 7. Assist with housing selection, housing assignments, and other Residential Life department initiatives and processes. 8. Facilitate student mediations, room changes and coordinate with Associate Director any changes to assignments. 9. Serve in the on-call administrative team responding to emergencies during and after hours as the first tier professional staff response. 10. Oversee the enforcement of all College rules and regulations on campus for all students. 11. Support the curricular model of the office, as well as the Division of Student Life. 12. Oversee respective area programming efforts and foster meaningful opportunities for student engagement. Administer area programming budget and tracking. 13. Assist with program and event implementation as needed by the Office of Student Life, Student Engagement and Campus Life. 14. Oversee the Class Councils for upper-class student representatives serving on the Student Government Association. 15. Monitor physical condition of building(s) on a regular basis and work collaboratively to identify and resolve problems. 16. Act as a representative for the department of Residential Life when engaging with campus partners, students and families. Student Life Functions: 1. Assist with New Student Orientation programs, Welcome Week, Move-In planning. 2. Serve as a Conduct Hearing Officer reporting to the Senior Associate Dean of Students for this responsibility 3. Manage the social host program including training students on how to responsibly host events. Again this should be coordinated by this person, but hosted by CC who does the training. Education and Skills Required Qualifications: 1. Bachelors degree required. 2. Previous experience in residential life preferred or other applicable experience required. 3. Capable of handling confidential/private information with high level of integrity. 4. Proven ability to build and sustain positive relationships with students, families, faculty and peers. 5. Must be available to live on/in campus housing, work various evening, overnight weekend and occasional holidays. 6. Valid driver licenses and ability to routinely travel to satellite housing approximately 2 miles from main campus. Desired Skills: 1. Well-organized, able to work under pressure, and adept at managing multiple projects 2. Ability to interact well with a variety of people from all aspects of the college and community. 3. Demonstrated commitment to collaboration, equity and inclusion. 4. Demonstrated ability to develop and deliver training programs. 5. Light travel necessary (Ex. Program shopping, on-call hospital follow-ups with students). 6. Must have excellent communications skills and ability to remain customer service centered when dealing in high pressure situations. Preferred Qualifications 1. Master’s degree preferred. 2. 2 or more years as a residence hall director, or experience working in Residential Life. 3. Commitment to foster inclusive residential communities, demonstrated experience or passion for supporting equity, diversity and inclusion initiatives. Physical Demands Driving Required Yes Salary Range $42,000-$44,765.76 Note Connecticut College is committed to fair and competitive compensation. The final offer will reflect the candidate’s experience, education, and the institution’s pay structure to ensure internal equity. Applicant Credentialing Thorough applicant credentialing will be conducted on the selected candidate which will include criminal records check, DMV check and depending on the position psychological exam and pre-placement physical. Posting Detail Information Open Date 10/19/2026 Applications accepted through Open Until Filled Yes Documents needed to Apply Required Documents Resume Cover Letter Optional Documents Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). Are you eligible to work in the U.S.? Yes No Do you have a valid drivers license? Yes No Will you now or in the future require sponsorship for employment visa status (e.g., H-1B visa)? Yes No To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://conncoll.peopleadmin.com/postings/1781": {
      "title": "Administrative Assistant",
      "description": "Connecticut College Portal | Administrative Assistant Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help About Connecticut College Administrative Assistant Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Position Details Position Information Position Title Administrative Assistant Department Walter Commons Pay Type Non Exempt Appointment Type (A) Continuing Full-Time Hours Per Week 37.5 Number of weeks 44 weeks per year other - # of weeks 22 Benefits Eligible Full Benefits Geographical Regional Qualifies for Relocation Reimbursement Yes Work Schedule Job Description General Scope of Duties The Administrative Assistant provides administrative, fiscal, records, and program support for the College’s global education operations in the Walter Commons for Global Study and Engagement (including Study Away) and the Center for International Studies and Liberal Arts ( CISLA ). The position supports the day-to-day operations of both areas by maintaining accurate records and spreadsheets, coordinating payments and expenditures, supporting events and programs, managing calendars and shared spaces, and providing administrative assistance to students, faculty, staff, visitors, and external partners. The position also has program-specific responsibilities related to CISLA and the Walter Commons, including supporting application and selection processes, maintaining program documentation, coordinating events and travel, and assisting with institutional reporting and compliance requirements. Approximately 50% of the work will be for each area and the position will report jointly to the Director of the Walter Commons for Global Study and Engagement and the Manager of Operations in CISLA . General Duties and Responsibilities Shared Administrative Responsibilities General Office Support: Serve as the primary point of contact for inquiries via phone, email, and in-person reception; direct students, faculty, and visitors to appropriate campus offices and resources. Financial & Purchasing Support: Process payment requests, p-card reconciliation, invoices, and expense reimbursements in coordination with Accounts Payable; support director and manager with routine budget tracking and expenditure monitoring. Event & Scheduling Coordination: Coordinate promotional materials, room reservations, catering, setup, and travel and lodging logistics for guest speakers, workshops, committee meetings, and program events. Records & Systems Management: Maintain digital filing systems, shared drives, databases, and compliance records in accordance with College data governance practices. Communications & Office Operations: Order and track supplies, manage incoming and outgoing mail, maintain shared calendars, and assist with the preparation of flyers, forms, and other promotional materials. CISLA Specific: Certificate Program Support: Provide logistical support for CISLA program, including scheduling ACTFL language testing, coordinating student presentations in language classes, and organizing certificate ceremonies and annual dinners. Admissions & Selection Support: Collect and organize applicant materials, faculty recommendations, and supporting documentation; schedule applicant interviews, prepare materials for selection committee meetings. Program Records & Communications: Coordinate the submission of CISLA senior integrative projects to the Digital Commons; assist with maintaining the CISLA website and updating program handbooks, newsletters, and promotional materials. Walter Commons Specific: Travel & Compliance Administration: Maintain the Walter Commons international travel registry; collect and track required student documentation, including waivers; and support Study Away Clery Act compliance and reporting. Grants & Specialized Fund Management: Track endowed funds and named grants, administer disbursements, and prepare end-of-semester financial summaries for program directors; process payments to international partners and vendors. Global Programs & Application Support: Maintain institutional partner records and MOUs; organize application materials for signature programs, named grants, and Study Away cycles; schedule committee reviews; and coordinate group travel logistics, including flight reservations and ground transportation. Education and Skills Associate’s degree required; bachelor’s degree preferred. 2–4 years of progressively responsible administrative and program coordination experience preferred, preferably in higher education, international education, nonprofit, or a similar environment. Knowledge of financial administration, including processing invoices, reimbursements, payment requests, purchasing card transactions, tracking expenditures, maintaining financial records, and assisting with budget reporting. Experience working with confidential or sensitive information and exercising appropriate discretion and judgment. Strong organizational, interpersonal, written, and verbal communication skills, with the ability to work effectively with students, faculty, staff, vendors, international partners, and individuals from diverse backgrounds and cultures. Proficiency with Microsoft Office/Microsoft 365, particularly Excel, Word, Outlook, and Teams, and the ability to learn and effectively use institution-specific systems and databases. Ability to independently manage multiple priorities and deadlines, identify and resolve routine administrative and financial issues, and appropriately escalate more complex matters. Ability to work independently while collaborating effectively with faculty, staff, students, and campus partners. Strong customer service orientation and commitment to supporting students and the College community. Commitment to cultural awareness, inclusivity, and supporting the College’s global education mission. Preferred Qualifications Physical Demands Driving Required No Salary Range $22.00-$25.00 Note Connecticut College is committed to fair and competitive compensation. The final offer will reflect the candidate’s experience, education, and the institution’s pay structure to ensure internal equity. Applicant Credentialing Thorough applicant credentialing will be conducted on the selected candidate which will include criminal records check, DMV check and depending on the position psychological exam and pre-placement physical. Posting Detail Information Open Date 10/28/2026 Applications accepted through Open Until Filled Yes Documents needed to Apply Required Documents Resume Cover Letter Optional Documents Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). Are you eligible to work in the U.S.? Yes No Will you now or in the future require sponsorship for employment visa status (e.g., H-1B visa)? Yes No To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://conncoll.peopleadmin.com/postings/1759": {
      "title": "Women’s Hockey Club Sports Coach",
      "description": "Connecticut College Portal | Women’s Hockey Club Sports Coach Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help About Connecticut College Women’s Hockey Club Sports Coach Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Position Details Position Information Position Title Women’s Hockey Club Sports Coach Department Student Activities/College Center -Group Pay Type Exempt Appointment Type (A) Continuing Part-Time Hours Per Week 4-12 Number of weeks Other (Benefits Ineligible) other - # of weeks 35 Benefits Eligible No Benefits Geographical Regional Qualifies for Relocation Reimbursement Yes Work Schedule Job Description General Scope of Duties Under the direction of the Assistant Director of Student Engagement and in cooperation with team leadership, the coach develops and implements all on ice instruction and strategy for the Club Hockey Program at Connecticut College. Connecticut College is a part of the American Collegiate Hockey Association ( ACHA ) and plays in the American Conference of the Northeast Collegiate Hockey Association. The team practices two nights and week at Dayton Ice Arena in New London, CT. The level of responsibility includes not only areas specific to the sport, but also a responsibility to the holistic well-being of the student-athletes involved. The outcomes of the program will be measured not only by the conventional means of measuring athletic success, but also by the ability of the coach to fulfill the mission of the College. This will be demonstrated in part by the mentoring of student-athletes to develop the wisdom and maturity necessary for positive citizenship and leadership. Length of employment is for the duration of the club hockey season, September-April, with an average of 4-12 hours of practice and game instruction per week, not including travel and other commitments. This is a stipend position of $3,000 per season. General Duties and Responsibilities 1. Instruct all aspects of on ice activity, including organization and direction of team practices, game coaching, and other duties appropriate to the program and the development of the student athletes. 2. Actively collaborate with team captains to design practice plans, instruct drills, oversee game strategies, and participate in all games and practices. 3. Work with the club’s executive board on scheduling and fundraising initiatives to provide a well-rounded experience for team members. 4. Develop and maintain discipline within the program, making referrals to the Office of Student Engagement when appropriate. 5. Develop and maintain a professional and active relationship with co-workers, faculty and staff, administration, alumni, friends of the college, and trustees. 6. Knowledgeable of, and adheres to, the rules and regulations of the NCAA , ACHA , NECHA , and any other applicable governing body. 7. Adheres to all policies of the Connecticut College student organization handbook and the employee handbook. Education and Skills Required Knowledge, Skills and Abilities In order to fully perform the above functions, the incumbent must possess the following knowledge, skills and abilities, or demonstrate that the major responsibilities of the job can be accomplished, with or without reasonable accommodation, using some combination of skills and abilities. 1. First-Aid, CPR , AED , & Blood Borne Pathogen certifications (must receive within 90 days of starting) 2. Effective leadership skills and motivational abilities with highly successful mentoring experiences. 3. Strong desire to work with and relate to student-athletes. 4. Sound administrative ability with emphasis on organization. 5. Effective communication skills, both written and verbal. 6. Positive style of leadership. 7. Willingness to travel for away games. 8. Sensitivity to cultural differences. Education and Experience Bachelor’s degree is preferred. Previous collegiate hockey coaching experience is preferred. Preferred Qualifications Physical Demands Driving Required Yes Salary Range $3000/Stipend Note Connecticut College is committed to fair and competitive compensation. The final offer will reflect the candidate’s experience, education, and the institution’s pay structure to ensure internal equity. Applicant Credentialing Thorough applicant credentialing will be conducted on the selected candidate which will include criminal records check, DMV check and depending on the position psychological exam and pre-placement physical. Posting Detail Information Open Date 10/19/2026 Applications accepted through Open Until Filled Yes Documents needed to Apply Required Documents Resume Cover Letter Optional Documents Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). Are you eligible to work in the U.S.? Yes No Do you have a valid drivers license? Yes No Will you now or in the future require sponsorship for employment visa status (e.g., H-1B visa)? Yes No To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://conncoll.peopleadmin.com/postings/1770": {
      "title": "Counselor",
      "description": "Connecticut College Portal | Counselor Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help About Connecticut College Counselor Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Position Details Position Information Position Title Counselor Department Student Counseling Services -Group Pay Type Exempt Appointment Type (A) Continuing Full-Time Hours Per Week 37.5 Number of weeks 40 weeks per year other - # of weeks 40 Benefits Eligible Full Benefits Geographical Regional Qualifies for Relocation Reimbursement No Work Schedule Job Description General Scope of Duties Join a close-knit liberal arts college community committed to the well-being, growth, and success of its students. The Counselor provides comprehensive clinical care, including assessment, diagnosis, counseling, and treatment, while also providing crisis intervention, consultation, on-call support, outreach, and psychoeducational programming. The position offers opportunities to train and supervise Psychology and Social Work interns and trainees and collaborate with campus partners to support student well-being. We seek a clinician with strong clinical judgment and experience providing affirming, sex-positive, culturally responsive, and trauma-informed care grounded in risk reduction and student self-determination. The ideal candidate is experienced in supporting students across diverse identities and lived experiences, including sexual orientation, gender identity and expression, race, class, religion, and physical functioning, and understands the intersection of these identities. This is an opportunity to build meaningful campus relationships and help create an environment where students feel seen, supported, and empowered to thrive. General Duties and Responsibilities 1. Perform psychological evaluations and treatment as required; report and discuss results with the team and requesting clinicians as needed. 2. Perform general and specialized clinical and outreach services in an affirmative, sex-positive, culturally sensitive approach consistent with risk reduction principles, and that promotes self-determination. 3. Ensure that documentation and productivity meet standards set in Student Counseling Services policy and procedure. 4. Provide training and supervision to clinical practicum students\\and interns as needed. 5. Remain current in literature and treatment developments in the mental health field, especially college student mental health, identity-focused issues (specifically LGBTQIA+ issues), and recovery. 6. Receive professional development as required to maintain license. 7. Participate in the development and updating of department policies and procedures that may affect students. 8. Perform administrative tasks that contribute to the functioning of the Student Counseling Services. 9. Participate in on-call rotation for emergency and crisis situations that pertain to students and campus life. 10. Collaborate with the Identity-focused Counselor to offer specific expertise regarding LGBTQIA+ identity issues to various components of the college community. 11. Adhere to the ethical codes of conduct appropriate to professional bodies such as the APA and other professional organizations. 12. Develop and maintain collaborative relationships with students, faculty, and staff. 13. Perform departmental research; manage and provide reports. 14. Work after hours and on breaks. 15. Other duties as assigned. Education and Skills 1. Doctoral degree in clinical psychology (Ph.D./ Psy.D), or Master’s in Social Work or Counseling. APA -approved program and internship. 2. 2-3 years of clinical experience required; experience in college mental health preferred. 3. Licensed to practice (or license-eligible) as a psychologist in the state of Connecticut. 4. High integrity needed to handle confidential information. 5. Knowledge, training and experience in working with diverse clinical and multicultural and other traditionally underserved populations (multicultural competence). 6. Demonstrated competence in LGBTQIA+ counseling and specialization in multicultural issues. 7. Experience in recovery, assessment and treatment of addictive behaviors (alcohol and other drugs, internet, eating disorders). 8. Well-organized, able to work under pressure and as a member of a team. 9. Ability to interact well with a variety of people from all aspects of the college and community. 10. Excellent presentation skills and social skills. 11. Travel as necessary to meet the requirements of the position. Preferred Qualifications Physical Demands Driving Required Yes Salary Range $60,220-65,000 Note Connecticut College is committed to fair and competitive compensation. The final offer will reflect the candidate’s experience, education, and the institution’s pay structure to ensure internal equity. Applicant Credentialing Thorough applicant credentialing will be conducted on the selected candidate which will include criminal records check, DMV check and depending on the position psychological exam and pre-placement physical. Posting Detail Information Open Date 10/21/2026 Applications accepted through Open Until Filled Yes Documents needed to Apply Required Documents Resume Cover Letter Optional Documents Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). Are you eligible to work in the U.S.? Yes No Will you now or in the future require sponsorship for employment visa status (e.g., H-1B visa)? Yes No To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://conncoll.peopleadmin.com/postings/1776": {
      "title": "Assistant Coach",
      "description": "Connecticut College Portal | Assistant Coach - Tennis Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help About Connecticut College Assistant Coach - Tennis Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Position Details Position Information Position Title Assistant Coach - Tennis Department Athletic Center -Group Pay Type Exempt Appointment Type (A) Temporary Full-Time Hours Per Week 37.5 Number of weeks 44 weeks per year other - # of weeks 44 Benefits Eligible Full Benefits Geographical Regional Qualifies for Relocation Reimbursement Yes Work Schedule Job Description General Scope of Duties Join Connecticut College Athletics as an Assistant Tennis Coach and help student-athletes grow, compete, and excel both on and off the court. Working closely with the Head Coach, you will play an important role in developing the women’s and men’s tennis programs by fostering a positive, inclusive, and competitive team culture that reflects the College’s mission and values. This position offers the opportunity to make a meaningful impact through athlete development, recruiting, and team engagement while supporting academic success, sportsmanship, and competitive excellence within the NESCAC and NCAA Division III . General Duties and Responsibilities General Duties and Responsibilities for Assistant Coach (75%): ● Assist in conducting a program that aligns with the mission and philosophy of Connecticut College, the department, NESCAC , and NCAA Division III . ● Prepare student-athletes for competition by planning for and conducting a regular schedule of fitness training, sport skill practices, scouting and competitions within NESCAC and NCAA regulations. ● Conduct oneself with the highest ethical and moral standards in regard to behavior on campus and in relationships with student-athletes and members of the campus community. ● Plan and execute dynamic warm-ups and cool-downs, practices, and competitive events. ● Maintain the highest ethical and professional standards in interactions with student-athletes and the campus community. ● Ensure a safe environment for all practices, competitions, and team travel. ● Comply with all applicable rules and policies (College, department, NESCAC , NCAA ). ● Support fundraising initiatives by engaging alumni and parents. ● Oversee and manage team social media and marketing efforts. ● In partnership with admissions, the head coach and in consultation with the athletic director conduct an effective student-athlete recruitment program within NESCAC and NCAA rules to ensure annual recruiting goals and target rosters are met each year to meet “competitive success goals” ● Communicate often with the Sports Medicine and Strength &amp; Conditioning staff about injured team members and coordinate treatment and rehabilitation as needed. ● Monitor and support student-athlete academic progress and overall well-being. ● Assist with individual meetings with student-athletes for performance and development. ● Maintain strong relationships with team alumni and families. ● Collaborate with Admissions and the Head Coach to meet annual roster goals through a compliant recruitment strategy to meet department competitive goals ● Communicate with Sports Medicine and Strength &amp; Conditioning staff regarding injury treatment and rehabilitation. ● Must be able to work evenings and weekends during the season and for recruitment. ● Must have a valid driver’s license with a clean driving record and be able to drive a 15-passenger vehicle. ● Foster a team culture that embraces diversity, equity, inclusion, and allyship. Administrative Duties (25%): ● Serve in administrative roles as assigned by the Athletic Directors. ● Provide event support for major athletic events including the Silfen Track &amp; Field Invitational, and NESCAC or NCAA championships, as needed. ● Additional administrative responsibilities may be assigned based on departmental needs. Education and Skills Bachelor’s Degree required and 1-3 years of collegiate playing and/or coaching experience. Individual must possess the skills and abilities or be able to teach and demonstrate all aspects of the sport they are coaching. The candidate must be able to work independently and have the ability to work evenings and weekends. Individuals must possess the knowledge, skills and abilities or be able to explain and demonstrate that the individual can perform the essential functions of the job, with or without reasonable accommodation, using some other combination of skills and abilities. The candidate should be considering a career in college coaching. Experience recruiting at the college level is also preferred. Preferred Qualifications Job Expectations: ● Work Location: This is an on-campus position and your office is located in the athletic center. Regular attendance at work is an essential job function ● Remote Work: Not permitted for full-time assistant coaches. ● Calendar Management: Maintain an updated Google Calendar reflecting all practices, competitions, recruiting, vacations, and PTO . The calendar must be shared with all athletic directors. ● Compensatory Time: If traveling for competition over a weekend (with Head Coach approval), you may take a comp day during the following week. This must be logged on your calendar and cannot be carried into future weeks. ● Time Reporting: All sick days, PTO , vacation, and Presidential Award days must be recorded in the DayForce timesheet system. Noncompliance may lead to disciplinary action. PTO Leave Policy: ● Annual Leave: 10 PTO days per academic year must be taken per the below guidance: Fall and Spring Coaches: PTO must be used during non-holiday winter or spring breaks; otherwise, coaches are expected to work 37.5 hours per week. Winter Coaches: PTO must be taken during the designated two-week spring break period. Mandatory Reporting: As a full-time Assistant Coach and Connecticut College employee, you are a mandated reporter. All concerns must be reported to the Athletic Directors or appropriate college authorities. Failure to comply may result in disciplinary action up to and including dismissal Physical Demands Driving Required Yes Salary Range $43,888 Note Connecticut College is committed to fair and competitive compensation. The final offer will reflect the candidate’s experience, education, and the institution’s pay structure to ensure internal equity. Applicant Credentialing Thorough applicant credentialing will be conducted on the selected candidate which will include criminal records check, DMV check and depending on the position psychological exam and pre-placement physical. Posting Detail Information Open Date 08/24/2026 Applications accepted through Open Until Filled Yes Documents needed to Apply Required Documents Resume Cover Letter Optional Documents Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). Are you eligible to work in the U.S.? Yes No Do you have a valid drivers license? Yes No Will you now or in the future require sponsorship for employment visa status (e.g., H-1B visa)? Yes No To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://conncoll.peopleadmin.com/postings/1786": {
      "title": "Budget Coordinator/Office Manager For Campus Life",
      "description": "Connecticut College Portal | Budget Coordinator/Office Manager For Campus Life Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help About Connecticut College Budget Coordinator/Office Manager For Campus Life Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Position Details Position Information Position Title Budget Coordinator/Office Manager For Campus Life Department Residential Life -Group Pay Type Exempt Appointment Type (A) Continuing Full-Time Hours Per Week 37.5 Number of weeks 52 weeks per year other - # of weeks 52 Benefits Eligible Full Benefits Geographical Regional Qualifies for Relocation Reimbursement No Work Schedule Job Description General Scope of Duties The Budget Coordinator/Office Manager provides comprehensive budget oversight, development, and administrative support exclusively for the Office of Campus Life, encompassing both Residence Life and Student Engagement. This role serves as a critical administrative hub, managing daily customer service functions, residential housing operations, key management, and departmental communications. The successful candidate will be detail- oriented, capable of proactive problem-solving, and able to communicate effectively with students, parents, faculty, and campus colleagues. General Duties and Responsibilities Housing and Access Management: 1. Provide secondary support for the distribution, tracking, inventory, and overall management of all residential keys. 2. Collaborate with Facilities to help process lock changes and keep key control documents up to date. 3. Assist with room changes by helping distribute keys and update ID card access as needed 4. Partner with the Associate Director and Director of Residential Life, to facilitate the end-of-year move-out process, including receiving/organizing documentation from residential life staff, assessing charges and compiling billing for final review. 5. Assist with various start, mid, and end-of-term audits and report preparation. 6. Conduct regular key audits, including taking key inventory and maintaining records. 7. Assist with housing move-in/move-out logistics, room turnover over and communication to students. 8. In consultation with the Director of Residential Life, develop and implement policies and procedures for the management of keys and key distribution. 9. Use housing software to ensure accurate record keeping, and verification for key distribution processing. Budget Management: 1. Responsible for budget management (maintain annual budgets, budget tracking strategies, and budget reports) for Office of Campus Life (Residential Life, Student Engagement) A. Prepare, process and file Request for Payment forms, Deposit Requests and other financial transactions B. Manage budget tasks (tax form preparation, accounts payable, student payroll et al) for all student group accounts. C. Advise student group treasurers on budget development and fiscal management D. Manage departmental Transact point of sale process. E. Process P-card transactions for student groups and reconcile account online as needed F. Distribute and reconcile cash advances for student groups G. Maintain and update departmental preferred vendor list and process appropriate purchase orders for student groups H. Collect, track, and monitor all program registration fees and revenue I. Serve a primary purchaser for CTW OneSource. 2. Student Clubs and Organizations A. Help to develop student organization policies for financial spending and budget management B. Serve as trainer and educator for the 80+ student organization about financial policies and procedures. C. Develop and deliver periodic training programs for student group leaders on all financial policies and procedures D. Serve a primary purchaser for CTW OneSource for student groups. E. Collect, code and process statements/invoices for payments 3. Student Managed Coffee Shops A. Serve as financial trainer/supervisor for student managed retail coffee locations on campus. i. Train, and develop financial procedures for the approximate thirty student baristas and managers working in the three locations. ii. Train students on use of point of sale system B. Lead contact with Accounting for credit card point of sale software systems. C. Develop and deliver periodic training programs for student managers on all financial policies and procedures D. Manage and organize all daily transactions and deposits. E. Collect, code and process statements/invoices for payments F. Prepare, process and file Request for Payment forms, deposit requests and other financial transactions Office of Campus Life Office Manager 1. Coordinate transportation requests for fleet vehicles, distribute and collect van keys, and manage reservations. 2. Communicate to student groups and leaders about proper protocols for transportation needs. 3. Update, develop, and coordinate the reservation process for student transportation services. 4. Provide excellent customer service for Campus Life related inquiries, via phone, email, and in-person interactions. 5. Serve as the frontline for the department, by managing communications through the shared residential life and student engagement email account, and phone service, with a high volume of emails from students, parents, and campus partners requesting information, and providing detailed outward answers. 6. Support the daily functions and operations of Campus Life 7. Collect, code and process statements/invoices for payment. 8. Manage and oversee all departmental purchase cards. 9. Monitor and track Office of Campus Life program registration processes and sign-ups. 10. Assist with logistical arrangements for Office of Campus Life programming excursions, which include working with outside vendors, hotels/conference centers, etc. 11. Perform all other duties and responsibilities as assigned or directed by the supervisor. Education and Skills 1. 1-3 years of relevant work experience 2. Associate’s Degree required 3. Bachelor’s degree preferred 4. Strong written and verbal communication skills 5. Able to interact well with a wide range of people from all aspects of the College and community 6. Proficient in Microsoft Office and Excel 7. High integrity needed to handle confidential information 8. Knowledge of general office equipment 9. Well-organized, able to work under pressure, detail-oriented and capable of proactive problem solving 10. No travel required Preferred Qualifications Physical Demands Driving Required No Salary Range $48,000-$52,000 Note Connecticut College is committed to fair and competitive compensation. The final offer will reflect the candidate’s experience, education, and the institution’s pay structure to ensure internal equity. Applicant Credentialing Thorough applicant credentialing will be conducted on the selected candidate which will include criminal records check, DMV check and depending on the position psychological exam and pre-placement physical. Posting Detail Information Open Date 10/28/2026 Applications accepted through Open Until Filled Yes Documents needed to Apply Required Documents Resume Cover Letter Optional Documents Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). Are you eligible to work in the U.S.? Yes No Will you now or in the future require sponsorship for employment visa status (e.g., H-1B visa)? Yes No To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    }
  },
